/// ReasoningEngineQuestionBank — 60 questions (53 single-select, 7 multi-select)
enum ReasoningEngineQuestionBank {
    static let all: [Question] = [
        Question(id: "1", question: "A Success Architect explains the runtime to a client as \"just one big LLM.\" Which correction best reflects the actual architecture?", options: [("A", "Daisy is a hybrid environment combining a deterministic resolver, an LLM reasoning loop, and a runtime trust layer wrapping every LLM call."), ("B", "Daisy is a single fine-tuned model that has been trained specifically on Salesforce CRM schemas and org metadata."), ("C", "Daisy is a rules engine only, and the LLM is invoked exclusively for voice channel deployments, not text channels."), ("D", "Daisy is a load balancer that routes user messages between multiple identical LLM instances for redundancy.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Official Agentforce architecture documentation describes the runtime as a three-layer hybrid: deterministic resolver, LLM reasoning loop, and Einstein Trust Layer.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: No such org-specific fine-tuning layer is described; the LLM is a general foundation model constrained by authored logic.  \n* C: The deterministic/LLM split applies to all channels, not just voice.  \n* D: There is no load-balancing redundancy concept in the architecture described."),
        Question(id: "2", question: "An architect is deciding what belongs in AgentScript vs. what the LLM should judge. Which statement correctly separates the two domains?", options: [("A", "The LLM controls all conditional branching, while AgentScript only stores static text with no runtime evaluation at all."), ("B", "Deterministic logic controls what the agent knows; the LLM controls whether and how to act on that knowledge."), ("C", "Deterministic logic and LLM judgment are fully interchangeable, since either layer can evaluate `if` conditions equally well."), ("D", "The LLM decides which variables to set, while AgentScript only decides which subagent responds to the user.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: This is the central framing of the deterministic/probabilistic split in Agentforce's reasoning architecture.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: AgentScript actively evaluates conditionals in Phase 1; it is not static text only.  \n* C: Only the deterministic resolver evaluates `if` conditions — the LLM never sees them.  \n* D: `set @variables.X` statements are deterministic and authored, not LLM-decided."),
        Question(id: "3", question: "A client asks why their agent behaves so predictably for tool-call sequencing but flexibly for phrasing. Which explains this duality? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Both tool sequencing and phrasing are controlled by the same FSM, which simply toggles between strict and lenient modes."), ("B", "Tool invocations use an FSM for retries, while phrasing flexibility comes from a secondary DAG that reorders sentences at random."), ("C", "Tool invocations are modeled as a DAG preventing loops, while subagent/state transitions use an FSM with retry (\"backward arrow\") tracking."), ("D", "Phrasing flexibility comes from the DAG structure, since DAGs allow multiple equally valid paths through a conversation.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: Agentforce models tool/action dependencies as a DAG and subagent/state transitions with retry logic as an FSM.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: A single toggling FSM is not how Agentforce's two distinct mathematical structures work.  \n* B: This reverses which structure applies to which mechanic and invents a nonexistent random reordering function.  \n* D: Phrasing flexibility comes from the LLM layer, not the DAG, which governs action dependency ordering."),
        Question(id: "4", question: "Which statement correctly distinguishes the authoring domain from the runtime domain?", options: [("A", "Both deploy and publish produce identical runtime artifacts; the commands exist only for CLI convenience and have no functional difference."), ("B", "Deploying the `AiAuthoringBundle` automatically creates the `GenAiPlannerBundle` and activates the agent for end users immediately."), ("C", "The `GenAiPlannerBundle` is created first during authoring, then converted into an `AiAuthoringBundle` when the agent is published."), ("D", "`sf agent publish` compiles the `.agent` file into the runtime `GenAiPlannerBundle`; deploy alone only stages the `AiAuthoringBundle`.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: Deploy stages the authoring bundle only; publish compiles the agent and creates the runtime graph including `GenAiPlannerBundle`.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Deploy and publish are functionally distinct steps, not identical or merely stylistic alternatives.  \n* B: Deploy does not create runtime entities or activate the agent — publish does the compiling.  \n* C: This reverses the actual sequence; authoring bundles come first, runtime bundles are compiled from them."),
        Question(id: "5", question: "During Phase 1, which of the following actions occur before any LLM call is made? (Choose 3)", options: [("A", "Evaluating `if`/`else if`/`else` conditions against current variable values."), ("B", "Executing `run @actions.X` calls synchronously and capturing their outputs."), ("C", "The LLM selecting which of several available tools best matches user intent."), ("D", "Filtering the tool schema using `available when` guards on each action."), ("E", "The LLM producing a terminal text response ending the reasoning loop.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1, 3], explanation: "Why correct: Condition evaluation, synchronous action execution, and `available when` filtering are all deterministic Phase 1 steps completed before the LLM is invoked.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Tool selection is a Phase 2 LLM decision, not a Phase 1 deterministic step.  \n* E: Producing a terminal response is the LLM's Phase 2 output, not part of Phase 1 resolution."),
        Question(id: "6", question: "An architect is debugging unexpected agent output. Which diagnostic question does the two-phase model let them ask precisely?", options: [("A", "Did the failure occur because the wrong branch was resolved in Phase 1, or because the LLM acted incorrectly on the right branch in Phase 2?"), ("B", "Was the failure caused by insufficient GPU throughput allocated to the underlying foundation model during that specific reasoning turn?"), ("C", "Did the failure occur in the Apex governor limit layer, since all reasoning failures ultimately trace back to Apex heap size?"), ("D", "Was the failure caused by a missing Named Credential, since all reasoning discrepancies stem from external authentication issues?")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: The two-phase execution model frames diagnosis as a binary: wrong branch shown (Phase 1) vs. right branch but wrong LLM action (Phase 2).\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: GPU throughput is not a diagnostic concept exposed to architects in this model.  \n* C: Not every reasoning failure is an Apex governor limit issue; many are pure Phase 1/2 logic issues.  \n* D: Named Credentials relate to specific action types, not the general two-phase diagnostic framing."),
        Question(id: "7", question: "What exactly does the LLM receive as input during Phase 2?", options: [("A", "The full raw `.agent` file text including all `if` blocks, so it can reason about which branch a human author intended."), ("B", "One system prompt, full conversation history, only the resolved pipe text matching current state, and schemas for actions passing `available when`."), ("C", "Only the current user's latest message, with no conversation history, to keep each reasoning turn stateless and independent."), ("D", "Two competing system prompts — the global one and any subagent override — merged together so the LLM can pick priorities.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Phase 2 input is precisely the resolved prompt, history, and filtered tool schema — never raw AgentScript syntax.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: The LLM never sees raw `if` blocks or AgentScript syntax at all.  \n* C: Full conversation history is included, not just the latest message.  \n* D: Subagent system overrides replace the global prompt entirely; they are never merged."),
        Question(id: "8", question: "Which of the following is true about how the LLM can end a Phase 2 reasoning iteration?", options: [("A", "It must always transition to another subagent, since terminal text responses are reserved for the `after_reasoning` block only."), ("B", "It can only ever call exactly one predetermined action per turn, with no ability to produce a terminal text response."), ("C", "It can call an action, produce a terminal response, or delegate to a subagent that is available as a tool."), ("D", "It can silently modify the `available when` conditions of other actions to unlock them for a future turn.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: There are exactly three LLM decisions: call an action, produce a terminal response, or delegate to a subagent.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Terminal responses are produced directly by the LLM in Phase 2, not reserved for `after_reasoning`.  \n* B: Terminal responses are a normal, available LLM decision, not excluded.  \n* D: The LLM cannot alter `available when` conditions; those are deterministic and authored."),
        Question(id: "9", question: "A developer places a post-action success check at the bottom of `reasoning.instructions: ->`. What is the resulting production symptom?", options: [("A", "The action underlying the check is automatically retried up to ten times regardless of variable state until it succeeds."), ("B", "The agent throws a hard compile-time error immediately, refusing to publish until the check is moved above the pipe text."), ("C", "The success check simply never executes at all, even after the loop exits and `after_reasoning` runs its own logic."), ("D", "The LLM re-sees the \"ask user for input\" instructions on every re-resolution before ever reaching the success check, causing double-execution.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: Because the whole instruction block re-resolves from the top on every iteration, a bottom-placed check is preceded by stale prompts every time.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: There is no ten-retry mechanism tied to check placement in AgentScript.  \n* B: Placement order does not cause a compile-time error — it is a silent logic bug, not a build failure.  \n* C: The check does eventually execute; the problem is the LLM sees confusing stale prompts first, not that it never runs."),
        Question(id: "10", question: "What is a \"backward arrow\" in the FSM model, and where can an architect visually inspect it?", options: [("A", "A retry edge recorded when a tool call fails and the LLM reasons into an alternative approach, visible in AgentLens trace graphs."), ("B", "A rollback of committed database transactions triggered automatically whenever any Apex governor limit is exceeded mid-turn."), ("C", "A UI arrow in Agent Builder that lets an architect manually rewind the conversation to an earlier user message."), ("D", "A visual indicator in Data Cloud showing which foundation model was billed for a given inference call.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Backward arrows are FSM retry edges recorded on tool-call failure, visualized as edges in AgentLens.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: This describes a database rollback mechanism, not the FSM retry concept.  \n* C: There's no manual conversation-rewind UI feature described as a \"backward arrow.\"  \n* D: Model billing visibility is a Gen AI Audit Log feature, unrelated to backward arrows."),
        Question(id: "11", question: "During the reasoning loop, roughly how many iterations can occur before the runtime forcibly exits back to the router?", options: [("A", "Exactly 1 iteration, since every reasoning loop is limited to a single LLM call per user turn by design."), ("B", "Approximately 3–4 iterations, as a protective guardrail against infinite loops."), ("C", "Up to 100 iterations, matching the org-wide maximum of active or committed agents."), ("D", "An unlimited number, since the loop only exits once a terminal response or explicit timeout error occurs.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: The platform enforces a hard guardrail of approximately 3–4 reasoning iterations per turn.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: A single iteration is too restrictive; multiple tool calls are explicitly supported before the cap.  \n* C: 100 is the unrelated org-wide limit on active/committed agents, not a per-turn iteration cap.  \n* D: The loop is explicitly bounded, not unlimited, precisely to prevent infinite loops."),
        Question(id: "12", question: "Which block runs exactly once per turn, strictly after the reasoning loop has produced a terminal text response?", options: [("A", "The Einstein Trust Layer's toxicity screening pass, which always executes after the user has already seen the response."), ("B", "`before_reasoning`, used to gate authentication and pre-load data prior to any LLM involvement."), ("C", "`after_reasoning`, used for cleanup, logging, or conditional transitions based on what the turn accomplished."), ("D", "`reasoning.instructions`, which re-resolves one final time after the terminal response has already been delivered to the user.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: `after_reasoning` fires once per turn, after the loop ends with a terminal response.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Trust Layer screening happens before the response reaches the user, not after.  \n* B: `before_reasoning` fires before the loop begins, not after a terminal response.  \n* D: `reasoning.instructions` re-resolves during the loop on each tool call, not as a final post-response step."),
        Question(id: "13", question: "A router subagent instructs the LLM \"Do not answer. Route only,\" while the global system prompt says \"Answer the user's questions helpfully.\" What results?", options: [("A", "The global system prompt is always ignored entirely once any subagent defines its own `reasoning.instructions` block."), ("B", "The subagent-level `reasoning.instructions` always silently overrides any conflicting global `system.instructions` content by design."), ("C", "The platform throws a BLOCK-level safety review finding and refuses to publish the agent until the contradiction is resolved."), ("D", "Undefined behavior, because the two contradicting instructions are both delivered to the LLM with no guaranteed resolution.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: This produces undefined behavior — one instruction wins, but you cannot predict which.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: The global system prompt still applies unless a subagent defines its own `system:` override, which is unrelated to `reasoning.instructions`.  \n* B: There is no rule that reasoning instructions automatically override contradicting global instructions.  \n* C: Contradictory tone instructions are a design smell, not a safety-review BLOCK category."),
        Question(id: "14", question: "A subagent defines its own `system:` block. What happens to the global `system.instructions` for that subagent?", options: [("A", "It is completely replaced — the subagent no longer has any global invariants unless they are explicitly restated."), ("B", "It is merged token-by-token with the subagent override, with global instructions always taking priority in any conflict."), ("C", "It remains fully active underneath the override, since overrides only apply to persona tone, not safety invariants."), ("D", "It is appended after the subagent's override text, so the LLM effectively receives both prompts back-to-back.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: A subagent-level system override completely replaces the global system instructions — it does not merge or append.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: There is no token-by-token merge mechanism; replacement is total, not selective.  \n* C: Safety invariants are not automatically preserved — they must be explicitly restated in the override.  \n* D: Appending is not how overrides behave; the global block is dropped entirely, not appended."),
        Question(id: "15", question: "A developer writes `if @variables.is_verified == True:` inside a `reasoning.instructions: |` block. What happens at runtime?", options: [("A", "The platform automatically detects the mistake and silently converts the block to `->` mode during compilation."), ("B", "The literal text \"if @variables.is_verified \\== True:\" is sent to the LLM as English prose, which may or may not follow it correctly."), ("C", "A compile-time error is thrown immediately, since conditional keywords are strictly forbidden inside `|` mode blocks."), ("D", "The condition is evaluated deterministically exactly as it would be in `->` mode, since `if` keywords always trigger Phase 1 resolution.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: In literal `|` mode, no evaluation occurs — the raw text including `if` keywords is passed to the LLM verbatim as prose.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: There is no automatic mode-conversion; this is a silent authoring bug that ships as written.  \n* C: No compile error occurs — this is exactly why it's known as \"the invisible bug.\"  \n* D: Deterministic evaluation only happens in `->` mode, not `|` mode, regardless of keywords used."),
        Question(id: "16", question: "Which statement correctly describes the `before_reasoning` and `after_reasoning` surfaces?", options: [("A", "`before_reasoning` supports `instructions: |` mode only, while `after_reasoning` supports `instructions: ->` mode only."), ("B", "Both require an `instructions: ->` wrapper identical to `reasoning.instructions`, since all AgentScript blocks share the same syntax."), ("C", "Neither supports an `instructions:` wrapper — content is written directly, and wrapping it causes a compilation error."), ("D", "Both blocks are processed by the LLM directly as system-level context, identical in function to a subagent system override.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: Both lifecycle hooks take direct content with no `instructions:` wrapper; using one is a documented compile error.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Neither block supports either literal or procedural wrapper syntax — both take direct, unwrapped content.  \n* B: This contradicts AgentScript's rule that these blocks do NOT use the `instructions:` wrapper.  \n* D: These blocks are processed by the Phase 1 deterministic resolver only; the LLM never sees them."),
        Question(id: "17", question: "A developer declares `is_verified: mutable boolean = false`. What happens at compile time?", options: [("A", "The declaration is accepted, but the variable becomes read-only for the remainder of the session immediately after declaration."), ("B", "The variable compiles successfully and behaves identically to `True`/`False` capitalization, since booleans are case-insensitive."), ("C", "The variable silently defaults to `True` instead of `False`, since lowercase literals are treated as the inverse value."), ("D", "A compile error occurs, because the parser rejects lowercase booleans — valid values must be capitalized `True`/`False`.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: AgentScript requires booleans to be capitalized `True`/`False`; lowercase is a common and explicit compile error.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Declaration syntax with a default value does not affect mutability; mutable variables remain writable.  \n* B: Booleans are explicitly case-sensitive in AgentScript, not case-insensitive.  \n* C: There's no silent value-inversion behavior described for lowercase literals — it's a hard error, not a workaround."),
        Question(id: "18", question: "A developer writes `set @variables.station = @inputs.station_name` immediately after the `run @actions.X` block that used `with station_name = ...` as an input. What happens?", options: [("A", "The assignment fails silently — `@inputs` is only valid inside `with` clauses during invocation, not after the action executes."), ("B", "The assignment succeeds normally, since `@inputs` values remain valid for the entire remainder of the current turn."), ("C", "The platform throws a visible runtime error immediately, halting the turn and surfacing a clear \"scope violation\" message to the architect."), ("D", "The assignment succeeds only if the action itself explicitly re-exports the input value as part of its outputs schema.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: `@inputs` values are valid only inside `with` clauses during invocation; using them afterward fails silently with no error.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: `@inputs` scope expires with the invocation; it does not persist for the rest of the turn.  \n* C: This is explicitly a silent failure — no visible error is thrown, making it dangerous.  \n* D: Re-exporting inputs as outputs is not a described mechanism for working around this scope issue."),
        Question(id: "19", question: "Which variable type must have a `source` attribute and must NOT declare a default value?", options: [("A", "Mutable variables, which always pull their initial value from a Named Credential rather than a literal default."), ("B", "Linked variables, which are read-only and populated from external session context such as `@MessagingSession`."), ("C", "`@outputs` variables, since their values come exclusively from action return payloads rather than static defaults."), ("D", "`@inputs` variables, since they are populated dynamically by the LLM's slot-filling process during Phase 2\\.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Linked variables are read-only, require a `source`, and must not declare a default value.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Mutable variables require a default value and have no concept of a Named Credential source.  \n* C: `@outputs` are not declared with `source`/default syntax at all — they are transient action-scoped values.  \n* D: `@inputs` are also transient and scoped to `with` clauses, not declared with a `source` attribute."),
        Question(id: "20", question: "A workflow needs to pass a customer's verification status from one subagent to another after a transition. What is the correct mechanism?", options: [("A", "Use `@outputs.is_verified` in the second subagent, since output scope extends across subagent boundaries indefinitely."), ("B", "Re-verify the customer from scratch in the second subagent, since variables never persist across subagent transitions."), ("C", "Rely on variable persistence — a mutable variable set in the first subagent remains fully available in the second after transition."), ("D", "Store the value only in `@inputs.is_verified`, since input scope is the only mechanism that survives a subagent transition.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: Variables persist across subagent transitions; this is the primary mechanism for passing data through multi-subagent workflows.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: `@outputs` scope expires immediately after the capturing `set`/`if` statements, long before any transition.  \n* B: This directly contradicts variable persistence, which holds explicitly across transitions.  \n* D: `@inputs` scope is even more limited, valid only during the originating action's invocation."),
        Question(id: "21", question: "What non-obvious side effect does `@utils.setVariables` have that differs from a normal LLM slot-fill on an action?", options: [("A", "It bypasses the Einstein Trust Layer entirely, since slot-filling utilities are exempt from runtime guardrail screening."), ("B", "It permanently locks the captured variables as read-only for the remainder of the session, converting them to linked variables."), ("C", "It automatically triggers `after_reasoning` to run twice, once for the capture and once for the following action."), ("D", "It ends the turn after capturing values, so any subsequent action in the same block will not fire in that same turn.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: `@utils.setVariables` ends the turn after capturing values; a subsequent action in the same block won't fire until the next turn.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: The Trust Layer wraps all LLM calls; there is no exemption for this utility.  \n* B: Captured variables remain mutable; they are not converted into read-only linked variables.  \n* C: There is no described double-firing of `after_reasoning` tied to this utility."),
        Question(id: "22", question: "An architect wants an action to always execute before the LLM reasons, regardless of what the LLM might decide. Which invocation mode achieves this?", options: [("A", "Deterministic invocation via `run @actions.X` in Phase 1, which always fires when that code path is reached."), ("B", "LLM-driven invocation via the `reasoning.actions` block, since the LLM always calls every listed action once per turn."), ("C", "Wrapping the action in `available when True`, since this forces the LLM to invoke it on the very first iteration."), ("D", "Declaring the action with `filter_from_agent: True`, which forces automatic execution before any reasoning begins.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: `run @actions.X` in Phase 1 always executes deterministically when its code path is reached, independent of LLM choice.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: LLM-driven actions are invoked only when the LLM decides to call them, not automatically every turn.  \n* C: `available when` only controls tool schema visibility; it does not force invocation.  \n* D: `filter_from_agent` controls what the LLM can display, not whether an action executes automatically."),
        Question(id: "23", question: "A refund action must never be callable unless the customer is verified. Which control provides a hard guarantee rather than a soft hint?", options: [("A", "A prose instruction such as \"only call this action if verified,\" placed in `reasoning.instructions`, trusting the LLM to comply."), ("B", "`available when @variables.is_verified == True`, which removes the action from the LLM's tool schema entirely when false."), ("C", "A comment in the `.agent` file reminding future developers that verification should be checked before this action is used."), ("D", "Setting the action's description to mention \"verified customers only\" so the LLM infers the requirement from context.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: `available when` is a hard compiler-level filter; when false, the action is completely absent from the tool schema.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Prose instructions are probabilistic — the LLM may or may not follow them, making this unsuitable for irreversible actions.  \n* C: Comments have no runtime effect whatsoever; they do not enforce anything.  \n* D: Description text is also just an interpretive hint, not an enforced constraint."),
        Question(id: "24", question: "Which two Apex requirements must be met for a class to be usable as an Agentforce action target? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "The class must use wrapper classes with `@InvocableVariable` annotations for all inputs and outputs."), ("B", "The class must avoid the reserved `@InvocableVariable` names `model`, `description`, and `label`."), ("C", "Input and output field names in the `.agent` file must exactly match the `@InvocableVariable` field names, including case."), ("D", "The class must be declared with the `global` access modifier rather than `public`.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Wrapper classes with `@InvocableVariable` fields are required, and three specific field names are reserved and will break agent compilation.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: While field name matching is real and important, it is a naming-consistency rule rather than one of the two selected structural Apex requirements being tested here.  \n* D: There's no described requirement that the class must be `global` rather than `public`."),
        Question(id: "25", question: "What is the \"zero-hallucination routing pattern,\" and how is it constructed?", options: [("A", "Disabling the LLM reasoning loop entirely for that action and replacing it with a hardcoded Apex-only response."), ("B", "Setting every action's description to include the word \"hallucination\" so the LLM is primed to avoid guessing at values."), ("C", "Combining `filter_from_agent: True` with `is_used_by_planner: True` so the LLM must call the action to get a value it can route on but never display."), ("D", "Using `available when False` permanently on the action so it never appears, forcing the router to use only variable-based logic.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: This exact flag combination prevents the LLM from hallucinating a value while still letting it reason and route on it.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: The pattern still relies on the LLM calling the action, not disabling reasoning entirely.  \n* B: There is no keyword-priming mechanism described; this is not how the pattern works.  \n* D: Permanently disabling an action removes it from use rather than creating a hallucination-safe routing value."),
        Question(id: "26", question: "What distinguishes \"Supervision\" delegation from a \"Handoff\" transition?", options: [("A", "Supervision and Handoff are two names for the exact same underlying `@utils.transition to` syntax, differing only by convention."), ("B", "In Supervision, the LLM is never involved in the decision; in Handoff, the LLM always decides when to transfer."), ("C", "Supervision only exists for voice modality agents, while Handoff is the only mechanism available for text channel agents."), ("D", "In Supervision, the child subagent runs its loop and returns control to the parent, which synthesizes the final response; in Handoff, control transfers completely and never returns.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: Supervision (`@subagent.X` reference) returns control to the parent; Handoff (`@utils.transition to`) is one-way with no return.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: They use distinct syntax (`@subagent.X` vs `@utils.transition to`) and behave differently, not interchangeably.  \n* B: Both mechanisms are LLM-driven decisions within `reasoning.actions`; this distinction is incorrect.  \n* C: Neither mechanism is restricted to a specific modality — both apply broadly."),
        Question(id: "27", question: "A regulated workflow requires zero LLM discretion about sequencing or routing. Which posture should the architect choose?", options: [("A", "Scripted posture, where all transitions use bare `transition to` and all actions use `run`, giving the LLM zero discretion."), ("B", "Agentic posture, since the LLM's flexibility is always preferable for regulatory compliance scenarios regardless of risk."), ("C", "Mixed posture, since combining LLM discretion with partial gates is always stricter than a fully scripted approach."), ("D", "Voice posture, since voice modality automatically enforces the strictest possible determinism on any workflow.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Scripted posture removes all LLM discretion via bare transitions and `run`\\-only actions, matching regulated, zero-variance requirements.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Agentic posture maximizes LLM flexibility, the opposite of what a zero-discretion regulatory requirement needs.  \n* C: Mixed posture still grants the LLM some discretion, which is not strict enough for a zero-discretion requirement.  \n* D: Voice is a modality, not a posture, and does not itself enforce determinism."),
        Question(id: "28", question: "Which of the following is NOT one of the five justifications for adding deterministic control?", options: [("A", "A regulatory requirement mandates specific sequencing or disclosure."), ("B", "The architect personally prefers deterministic code because it feels more professional in a client demo."), ("C", "An action is irreversible, such as a refund or delete."), ("D", "A specific behavior has failed in production and determinism is the fix.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Personal preference is explicitly not one of the five justifications; determinism must be justified by concrete need, not taste.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Regulatory requirement is explicitly one of the five listed justifications.  \n* C: Irreversible action is explicitly one of the five listed justifications.  \n* D: Observed production failure is explicitly one of the five listed justifications."),
        Question(id: "29", question: "Which three control primitives implement mixed posture? (Choose 3)", options: [("A", "`available when` guards that hard-remove actions from the tool schema based on a condition."), ("B", "Parameter pinning, binding a parameter to `@variables.X` instead of `...` to remove LLM slot-fill discretion."), ("C", "Conditional instructions using `if/else` in `reasoning.instructions: ->` so the LLM sees only the matching branch."), ("D", "Randomized response temperature adjustment applied per subagent to intentionally vary LLM creativity levels.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1, 2], explanation: "Why correct: These are the three named control primitives for mixed posture in Agentforce.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* D: Randomized temperature adjustment per subagent is not a described control primitive in this framework."),
        Question(id: "30", question: "Why is it recommended to keep instructions minimal and push logic into actions instead?", options: [("A", "AgentScript has a hard 500-character limit per `reasoning.instructions` block that cannot be exceeded under any circumstance."), ("B", "Shorter instructions reduce the per-action credit cost, since credits are billed per character of instruction text processed."), ("C", "Shorter, focused instructions improve LLM performance and help stay within the \\~10,000 token heuristic; action logic is also reusable across subagents."), ("D", "Moving logic into actions is required because the LLM cannot process any conditional logic longer than one sentence.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: Minimal instructions improve performance and aid the token heuristic, and action logic is reusable across subagents.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: No such hard 500-character limit exists in AgentScript.  \n* B: Credit billing is per action execution, not per character or token of instruction text.  \n* D: This mischaracterizes LLM capability; the concern is performance and clarity, not a strict sentence-length ceiling."),
        Question(id: "31", question: "A team is building their very first Agentforce implementation with a single clear domain. Which pattern should they default to?", options: [("A", "Post-Action Re-Resolution, since this pattern is required as the entry point for any new agent build."), ("B", "Router-First, since every agent implementation should begin with a routing layer regardless of domain count."), ("C", "Verification Gate, since all agents must verify identity before any other pattern can be safely applied."), ("D", "Single Scope — one `start_agent`, minimal or no subagents, no routing layer, unless multiple genuine domains exist.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: The minimalist architecture principle is to start with Single Scope and add complexity only when justified.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Post-Action Re-Resolution is a specific conditional-follow-up pattern, not a mandatory entry point.  \n* B: Router-First is only needed for genuinely separate domains, not as a universal default starting point.  \n* C: Verification Gate is only needed when protecting PII or irreversible actions, not universally required."),
        Question(id: "32", question: "In a Router-First pattern, what is the router subagent's only recommended job?", options: [("A", "Route the request to the correct domain subagent — it should not attempt to answer questions itself."), ("B", "Answer simple questions directly while delegating only complex questions to domain subagents."), ("C", "Pre-load all customer data for every domain subagent before any routing decision is made."), ("D", "Perform identity verification for every domain before routing, functioning as a universal verification gate.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: The router's only job is routing — it does not answer questions.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Answering any questions directly blurs the router's single responsibility and contradicts the recommended pattern.  \n* C: Pre-loading all domains' data is not the router's role and would be wasteful.  \n* D: Verification is handled by a dedicated Verification Gate pattern, not folded into the router by default."),
        Question(id: "33", question: "Where must a \"did the action succeed?\" conditional check be placed to correctly implement the Post-Action Re-Resolution pattern?", options: [("A", "At the bottom of the block, after all first-entry prompts, so the LLM always sees the greeting first."), ("B", "At the top of the `instructions: ->` block, so it fires immediately on every re-resolution including after tool calls."), ("C", "Inside the `after_reasoning` hook only, since result checks cannot be placed inside `reasoning.instructions` at all."), ("D", "Inside the action's own Apex class, since AgentScript cannot express any post-action conditional logic.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Because the block re-resolves top-to-bottom after each tool call, the success check must be at the top to fire immediately.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Bottom placement causes the LLM to see stale first-entry prompts before the check, the exact re-resolution trap.  \n* C: Result checks belong in `reasoning.instructions`, not exclusively in `after_reasoning`.  \n* D: AgentScript explicitly supports post-action conditionals via `if` statements in `reasoning.instructions`."),
        Question(id: "34", question: "What is the \"After-Reasoning Router Transition Trap,\" and how is it fixed?", options: [("A", "The `after_reasoning` block never fires after any transition, causing all state to be lost; the fix is duplicating all logic into `before_reasoning`."), ("B", "The router transitions to itself infinitely, causing an infinite loop; the fix is adding a hard-coded 3-second delay between transitions."), ("C", "A gate subagent transitions to a router mid-turn while the user's original setup message is still active, causing misrouting; the fix is instructing the router to recognize post-setup states and not route the stale message."), ("D", "The router always ignores `available when` guards after any transition, exposing restricted actions; the fix is removing the router subagent entirely.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: This exactly matches the documented trap and its recommended fix (router awareness of post-setup states).\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: `after_reasoning` does fire after a terminal response; the trap is about same-turn transitions, not lost state.  \n* B: No infinite self-transition or delay-based fix is described for this trap.  \n* D: `available when` guards are unaffected by this trap; removing the router is not the described fix."),
        Question(id: "35", question: "Which of the following operations cost 20 credits per invocation? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "An Apex action invocation."), ("B", "A Flow action invocation."), ("C", "`@utils.transition`."), ("D", "`if`/`else` control flow evaluation in Phase 1\\.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Apex and Flow actions each cost 20 credits per invocation, per the credit model table.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: `@utils.transition` is a free framework operation with zero credit cost.  \n* D: Deterministic `if`/`else` control flow in Phase 1 is free, costing zero credits."),
        Question(id: "36", question: "A client asks whether shortening their agent's instructions will reduce their monthly credit bill. What is the accurate answer?", options: [("A", "Yes — but only for voice-modality agents, since voice pricing uses token-based billing while text uses flat-rate billing."), ("B", "Yes — every token processed by the LLM is billed individually, so shorter instructions directly lower the credit bill."), ("C", "No — credits are billed strictly per user session regardless of instruction length or number of actions called."), ("D", "No — credit billing is per action execution, not per token; shortening instructions helps latency and accuracy under the 10k heuristic, not cost.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: This explicitly warns against conflating token cost (latency/accuracy) with credit cost (per action execution).\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: There's no described voice-only token-based billing distinction in the credit model.  \n* B: This is the exact conflation architects are warned against — credits are not billed per token.  \n* C: Credits scale with action executions per turn, not a flat per-session rate."),
        Question(id: "37", question: "An architect is estimating worst-case cost for a new agent. Data that is ALWAYS needed by every reasoning iteration should be handled how?", options: [("A", "Pre-loaded deterministically in `before_reasoning`, even though it costs 20 credits, to eliminate the LLM needing to call it during the loop."), ("B", "Left entirely to the LLM to fetch on-demand every single iteration, since always-needed data is cheaper when fetched repeatedly."), ("C", "Stored permanently as a linked variable with no `source`, since always-needed data should never require a fresh action call."), ("D", "Retrieved only through `@utils.setVariables`, since that utility is free and can substitute for any data-loading action.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Deterministic pre-loading in `before_reasoning` is recommended for data that is always needed, despite the 20-credit cost, since it avoids redundant LLM-driven calls.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Repeated on-demand fetching for always-needed data is wasteful, not cheaper.  \n* C: Linked variables must have a `source` and are for session context, not general data pre-loading.  \n* D: `@utils.setVariables` is for LLM slot-filling, not data-loading actions, and cannot substitute for a data-fetch action."),
        Question(id: "38", question: "Which limit governs the maximum size of a single agent response payload?", options: [("A", "32k tokens per agent response, matching the backend transformed plan trace limit."), ("B", "1,048,576 bytes (1M"), ("B", "per agent response."), ("C", "10,000 tokens per agent response, matching the architectural context heuristic."), ("D", "100 responses per org, matching the maximum active/committed agents limit.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: The platform specifies a 1MB (1,048,576 byte) maximum response size per agent response.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: 32k tokens is the backend transformed plan trace limit, a separate internal processing limit.  \n* C: 10,000 tokens is a performance heuristic for context budget, not a response size limit.  \n* D: 100 is the maximum number of active/committed agents per org, unrelated to response payload size."),
        Question(id: "39", question: "During which deployment phase is the `GenAiPlannerBundle` actually created?", options: [("A", "Phase 3: Metadata Deployment, since deploying the `AiAuthoringBundle` automatically generates all runtime bundles."), ("B", "Phase 2: Draft Validation, since `sf agent validate` compiles and instantiates all runtime metadata."), ("C", "Phase 4: Publish, which compiles the agent and creates the full runtime graph."), ("D", "Phase 6: Activate and Test, since activation is what compiles the agent into its runtime form.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: Publish is explicitly the phase where the agent compiles and `GenAiPlannerBundle` is created.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Metadata deployment only stages the authoring bundle; it does not generate runtime bundles.  \n* B: Validation only checks syntax and structure; it does not compile runtime bundles.  \n* D: Activation enables a previously published agent; it does not perform the compilation step."),
        Question(id: "40", question: "A client wants their agent to \"never admit to being AI\" to sound more human. What does the safety review classify this as?", options: [("A", "Not a safety review concern at all, since persona design is entirely outside the review's seven categories."), ("B", "A WARN-level finding under Fairness, which can be approved with a documented business justification."), ("C", "A WARN-level finding under Scope Boundaries, resolved by narrowing the agent's business domain."), ("D", "A BLOCK-level finding under Identity Transparency, with no business justification able to bypass it.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: Identity transparency is a BLOCK-level category with no override — the agent must identify as AI when asked.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Identity transparency is explicitly one of the seven safety review categories, and a BLOCK one at that.  \n* B: Fairness concerns discriminatory outcomes, not identity concealment, and is WARN-level, which doesn't apply here.  \n* C: Scope boundaries concern acting outside business domain, unrelated to identity concealment."),
        Question(id: "41", question: "Which ordering rule governs Phase 3 (Metadata Deployment)?", options: [("A", "Action implementations (Apex, Flows) must be deployed first, then the `AiAuthoringBundle` is deployed afterward."), ("B", "The `AiAuthoringBundle` must always be deployed first, with action implementations following in any subsequent release."), ("C", "Both must be deployed simultaneously in a single transaction, or the deployment will always fail with a dependency error."), ("D", "Order does not matter, since the platform automatically resolves dependency ordering during any deployment.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Action implementations must be deployed before the `AiAuthoringBundle`.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: This reverses the required deployment order.  \n* C: Simultaneous single-transaction deployment is not the described mechanism; sequencing matters.  \n* D: Order does matter — this is a critical ordering rule, not an automatically resolved concern."),
        Question(id: "42", question: "What must the Release Gate (Phase 5) include before an agent is activated? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "At least one live preview session using realistic (not trivial happy-path) utterances."), ("B", "A named human who explicitly reviews and approves activation."), ("C", "Automatic activation as soon as `sf agent validate` returns a passing result with zero errors."), ("D", "A mandatory rewrite of the agent's persona to remove all humor or personality traits.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: The Release Gate requires realistic live preview testing and explicit human approval before activation.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Validation passing checks syntax only; it does not substitute for the human-approved release gate.  \n* D: There is no requirement to strip personality traits as part of the release gate."),
        Question(id: "43", question: "What is the core response-time constraint that shapes all voice authoring rules?", options: [("A", "Every response must be under 500 tokens total, matching half of the general 10k token heuristic."), ("B", "Every response must be speakable in under 2 seconds, since TTS reads everything aloud including any structural markup."), ("C", "Every response must include exactly one filler phrase, regardless of whether an action call is involved."), ("D", "Every response must avoid using the `modality voice:` block, since that block is reserved for telephony-only deployments.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: The 2-second speakable constraint is the core driver behind all voice authoring rules like sentence length caps and no markdown.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: There's no 500-token hard cap specifically for voice responses.  \n* C: Filler phrases are recommended specifically around action calls to mask latency, not on every single response.  \n* D: The `modality voice:` block is exactly how voice deployments are configured, not something to avoid."),
        Question(id: "44", question: "Which three of these are among the eight voice latency anti-patterns? (Choose 3)", options: [("A", "Chained callouts — multiple sequential external API calls within a single turn."), ("B", "Bulky data retrievals that pull far more data into context than the turn actually needs."), ("C", "Over-routing — too many subagent transitions occurring within a single turn."), ("D", "Using the `connection customer_web_client:` block, which is inherently incompatible with any voice deployment.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1, 2], explanation: "Why correct: These three are explicitly listed among the eight voice latency anti-patterns.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* D: `connection customer_web_client:` is a standard way to deploy voice agents, not an anti-pattern."),
        Question(id: "45", question: "Why must URLs and citation references never be read aloud in a voice deployment?", options: [("A", "They exceed the 1MB response size limit whenever more than one URL is included in a single turn."), ("B", "They trigger the Einstein Trust Layer's toxicity screening, which blocks any response containing a URL pattern."), ("C", "They are incomprehensible in voice — TTS rendering of raw links or citations produces noise the user cannot use."), ("D", "They cause the FSM to record a backward arrow, treating the URL as a failed tool-call retry signal.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: URLs and citations are incomprehensible when spoken aloud by TTS.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: A URL string is nowhere close to the 1MB response size limit; this is an unrelated constraint.  \n* B: There is no described link between URLs and toxicity screening triggers.  \n* D: Backward arrows relate to failed tool-call retries, not to the presence of URLs in text."),
        Question(id: "46", question: "What is the recommended fix for the \"chained callouts\" anti-pattern in voice deployments?", options: [("A", "Disable the Einstein Trust Layer for that subagent only, since screening overhead is what causes the perceived chain latency."), ("B", "Convert every callout into a `@utils.transition to` call, since framework operations are always faster than external callouts."), ("C", "Increase the reasoning loop iteration cap from 4 to at least 10, giving each callout more room to complete sequentially."), ("D", "Batch the calls externally or pre-load the needed data in `before_reasoning` rather than chaining sequential external calls mid-turn.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: The recommended fix for chained callouts is batching externally or pre-loading in `before_reasoning`.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: There's no option to disable the Trust Layer per subagent, and it isn't described as the latency source here.  \n* B: `@utils.transition to` is a routing mechanism, not a substitute for an external data callout.  \n* C: Raising the iteration cap is not a described fix and isn't how the platform's guardrail works."),
        Question(id: "47", question: "An organization without Data Cloud provisioning wants to use ADL for knowledge grounding. What is the outcome?", options: [("A", "They cannot use ADL at all, since ADL requires Data Cloud provisioning as a hard prerequisite."), ("B", "They can still use the `KNOWLEDGE` source type only, since it does not depend on Data Cloud provisioning."), ("C", "They can use ADL fully, since Data Cloud is only required for the `RETRIEVER` source type, not `SFDRIVE` or `KNOWLEDGE`."), ("D", "They can use ADL with reduced relevancy ranking quality, since Data Cloud only affects ranking precision, not availability.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: ADL requires Data Cloud provisioning as a stated hard requirement; organizations without it cannot use ADL.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: `KNOWLEDGE` also depends on the broader ADL/Data Cloud requirement, not an exception to it.  \n* C: All three source types operate under the ADL/Data Cloud requirement.  \n* D: The requirement is availability, not merely a degradation in ranking quality."),
        Question(id: "48", question: "Which ADL source type requires the CRM Connector to be active and articles to be published (not draft)?", options: [("A", "`SFDRIVE`, which indexes files from Salesforce Files/Content such as PDFs."), ("B", "`KNOWLEDGE`, which indexes published Salesforce Knowledge Articles."), ("C", "`RETRIEVER`, which indexes custom vector search endpoints requiring external infrastructure."), ("D", "`EXTERNALSERVICE`, which indexes REST APIs registered via External Services.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: `KNOWLEDGE` explicitly requires an active CRM Connector and published (not draft) articles.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: `SFDRIVE` requires file accessibility to the agent user, not CRM Connector or article publication status.  \n* C: `RETRIEVER` requires external vector search infrastructure, unrelated to CRM Connector or Knowledge Article publication.  \n* D: `EXTERNALSERVICE` is not one of the three ADL source types described (SFDRIVE, KNOWLEDGE, RETRIEVER)."),
        Question(id: "49", question: "Where does the dynamic retriever token `{!$EinsteinSearch:sfdc_ai__DynamicRetriever.results}` belong?", options: [("A", "Inside the `knowledge:` block's `sources:` list, as a fourth valid `source_type` alongside SFDRIVE, KNOWLEDGE, and RETRIEVER."), ("B", "Directly inside `reasoning.instructions: ->` in the `.agent` file, immediately above the relevant `if` condition."), ("C", "Inside the Prompt Template body in Prompt Builder — not directly in the `.agent` file's `reasoning.instructions`."), ("D", "Inside the Apex wrapper class as an `@InvocableVariable`, since it must be exposed as a formal action input.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: This token is placed in the Prompt Template body, which the `.agent` file only references by name via `generatePromptResponse://`.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: It is a template-body token, not a `source_type` enum value for the `knowledge:` block.  \n* B: Placing it directly in `reasoning.instructions` is explicitly called out as the common syntax gotcha to avoid.  \n* D: It is a Prompt Builder templating token, not an Apex `@InvocableVariable`."),
        Question(id: "50", question: "Which three items belong on the ADL setup checklist? (Choose 3)", options: [("A", "Data Cloud is provisioned in the org."), ("B", "Knowledge Articles are published, since draft articles are not indexed."), ("C", "The agent user has the `AllowViewKnowledge` permission."), ("D", "The `available when` guard is set to `True` on every knowledge action regardless of context.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1, 2], explanation: "Why correct: These three are explicit items on the ADL setup checklist.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* D: Blanket-setting `available when True` on every knowledge action is not part of the checklist and defeats the purpose of conditional gating."),
        Question(id: "51", question: "A `FunctionStep` in a trace shows no output capture in the `postVars` diff, even though the action completed successfully. What does this most likely indicate?", options: [("A", "The FSM recorded a backward arrow, indicating the LLM chose not to use the action's output at all."), ("B", "The Einstein Trust Layer blocked the output due to a toxicity detection false positive on the returned data."), ("C", "The action exceeded the 1MB response size limit, causing the platform to silently truncate the output."), ("D", "An `@inputs` or `@outputs` scope violation — the variable was referenced outside its valid scope, causing a silent failure.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: This is the diagnostic signature of an `@inputs`/`@outputs` scope violation.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: A backward arrow indicates a retry after failure, not a `postVars` capture issue for a successfully completed action.  \n* B: Toxicity screening operates on LLM-facing content, not a described cause of missing `postVars` capture.  \n* C: 1MB truncation is a size limit issue, not the described cause of this specific symptom."),
        Question(id: "52", question: "Which trace step shows the exact prompt sent to the LLM, the tools offered, and the LLM's raw decision?", options: [("A", "`LLMStep`, which captures `messages_sent`, `tools_sent`, and `response_messages`."), ("B", "`FunctionStep`, which only ever records action inputs and outputs, never prompt content."), ("C", "`NodeEntryStateStep`, which records only which subagent is currently active."), ("D", "`EnabledToolsStep`, which records only the post-filtering list of available tools without any prompt content.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: `LLMStep` is explicitly described as capturing the full prompt, tool schema sent, and the LLM's raw decision.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: `FunctionStep` covers action execution details, not the LLM prompt or decision itself.  \n* C: `NodeEntryStateStep` only shows active subagent and variable state at entry, not prompt content.  \n* D: `EnabledToolsStep` shows filtered tool availability but not the full prompt or LLM decision."),
        Question(id: "53", question: "An agent keeps asking the user for information they already provided earlier in the conversation. Where should the architect look first, and what is the likely cause?", options: [("A", "`FunctionStep.preVars`, checking Apex governor limits — the likely cause is a SOQL query row limit being exceeded silently."), ("B", "`LLMStep.messages_sent`, checking whether the variable was actually injected — the likely cause is the variable was never set or the `{!@variables.X}` syntax is wrong."), ("C", "`TransitionStep`, checking routing history — the likely cause is the agent transitioning to the wrong subagent every single turn."), ("D", "`ReasoningStep.category`, checking for `UNGROUNDED` — the likely cause is a knowledge grounding failure on an unrelated ADL source.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: This exact symptom-to-cause mapping (repeated info requests → check prompt injection → variable/syntax issue) is a standard diagnostic pattern.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Governor limits are unrelated to this specific \"already provided info\" symptom in the diagnostic map.  \n* C: Routing history relates to wrong-subagent symptoms, not repeated information requests.  \n* D: Grounding failures relate to unhelpful post-gate responses, a different listed symptom."),
        Question(id: "54", question: "For rapid, turn-by-turn visual debugging during development — including seeing backward retry arrows as graph edges — which tool should an architect reach for first?", options: [("A", "The Gen AI Audit Log in Data Cloud, since it is designed specifically for turn-by-turn visual debugging during development."), ("B", "Raw CLI `jq` queries against production STDM logs, since visual tools cannot render backward arrows."), ("C", "AgentLens (or the native Agent Builder trace visualizer), for visual Chain-of-Thought inspection."), ("D", "The Salesforce Setup Apex Debug Log viewer, since AgentScript execution is logged identically to standard Apex trace logs.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: AgentLens is explicitly recommended first for rapid visual diagnosis, including rendering backward arrows as graph edges.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: The Gen AI Audit Log is a compliance/model-usage log, not a turn-by-turn visual debugger.  \n* B: `jq` queries are recommended for scripted, production-scale extraction, not rapid visual development debugging.  \n* D: AgentScript execution is not logged as standard Apex debug logs; it has its own distinct trace model."),
        Question(id: "55", question: "Three `model_config` blocks are defined: at the org level, the agent level, and a specific subagent level, each specifying a different model. Which one applies to that subagent at runtime?", options: [("A", "All three are merged into a single ensemble model call, blending responses from each configured model."), ("B", "The org-level `model_config`, since organizational defaults always take final precedence over narrower overrides."), ("C", "The agent-level `model_config`, since it is the middle tier and always wins ties between org and subagent settings."), ("D", "The subagent-level `model_config`, since precedence is subagent \\> agent \\> org.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: Precedence is explicitly subagent overrides agent, which overrides org default.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: There is no ensemble-blending mechanism described; exactly one model config applies per subagent.  \n* B: Org-level is the lowest-precedence default, not the final authority when narrower configs exist.  \n* C: Agent-level is overridden by a more specific subagent-level config when one is present."),
        Question(id: "56", question: "Why is HyperClassifier specifically recommended for the routing subagent in a multi-model setup?", options: [("A", "It is purpose-built for intent classification, extremely fast and cheap, reducing turn latency on routing decisions."), ("B", "It is the only model capable of executing `run @actions.X` calls deterministically without LLM involvement."), ("C", "It is required by the platform for any subagent that uses `available when` guards on its actions."), ("D", "It is the only model compatible with the Einstein Trust Layer's zero-retention policy enforcement.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: HyperClassifier is recommended for routing specifically because it's fast, cheap, and purpose-built for classification.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: `run @actions.X` is a deterministic Phase 1 mechanic unrelated to which LLM model is configured.  \n* C: `available when` guards are unrelated to model selection; they apply regardless of the configured model.  \n* D: The Trust Layer wraps all LLM calls regardless of which specific model is configured."),
        Question(id: "57", question: "Which four layers does the A2A protocol define for enabling communication between Agentforce and third-party agents? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Authentication, Billing, Data Cloud Indexing, and Voice Modality."), ("B", "Capability Discovery, Task and Execution, Communication, and Security."), ("C", "DAG Construction, FSM Transition, Backward Arrow Logging, and Trace Visualization."), ("D", "Deterministic Resolution, LLM Reasoning, Trust Layer Screening, and Response Delivery.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: These are the exact four A2A layers: Capability Discovery, Task and Execution, Communication, and Security.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Billing and Voice Modality are unrelated concepts not part of the A2A protocol's four layers.  \n* C: These terms describe internal Atlas engine mechanics, not the A2A interoperability protocol.  \n* D: This describes the internal two-phase turn anatomy, not the A2A protocol layers."),
        Question(id: "58", question: "What is the key architectural difference between A2A and MCP?", options: [("A", "MCP defines agent-to-agent communication, while A2A defines tool and service connectivity for external systems."), ("B", "A2A and MCP are competing, mutually exclusive protocols, and an org must choose exactly one to implement."), ("C", "A2A defines how agents talk to each other; MCP defines how an agent connects to external tools/services exposed as MCP servers."), ("D", "Both protocols are fully GA today and recommended as the primary integration mechanism over Apex callouts.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: A2A is agent-to-agent communication, while MCP is agent-to-external-tool connectivity — complementary, not competing.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: This reverses the actual definitions of A2A and MCP.  \n* B: They are complementary protocols, not mutually exclusive alternatives.  \n* D: Both are emerging/developer-preview capabilities, with current production systems advised to use Apex callouts and External Services instead."),
        Question(id: "59", question: "An architect reviews an agent and finds `@utils.setVariables` immediately followed by an action call relying on the just-captured variable, both inside the same `reasoning.actions` block, expecting both to fire in one turn. What is wrong, and what is the fix?", options: [("A", "The problem is that the captured variable must be renamed to avoid colliding with the reserved word `model`, `description`, or `label`."), ("B", "Nothing is wrong — both will fire correctly in sequence within the same turn as authored, since actions execute in declared order."), ("C", "The problem is that `setVariables` is a Phase 1 deterministic operation and cannot appear inside `reasoning.actions` at all, causing a compile error."), ("D", "`setVariables` ends the turn after capturing values, so the action never fires that turn; the fix is to let the LLM slot-fill the action directly using `...` instead.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: `@utils.setVariables` is a turn-ending framework operation — once it captures values, the reasoning loop ends for that turn, so a subsequent action in the same block cannot fire until the next turn. Letting the action's own slot-fill (`...`) extract the value avoids the turn boundary entirely.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: The reserved-name issue applies to Apex `@InvocableVariable` fields, unrelated to this turn-ending side effect.  \n* B: This is precisely the incorrect assumption the anti-pattern warns against — the second action will not fire that turn.  \n* C: `setVariables` is a valid `reasoning.actions` entry; it does not cause a compile error by being placed there."),
        Question(id: "60", question: "An architect justifies trimming an agent's instructions by telling the client \"this will lower our monthly credit bill.\" Which statement correctly evaluates that justification?", options: [("A", "The justification is accurate: instruction length is billed per character processed by the LLM, so shorter instructions directly cut the credit bill."), ("B", "The justification is flawed: credit billing is per action execution, not per token or character; shorter instructions improve latency and accuracy under the token budget heuristic, not cost."), ("C", "The justification is accurate, but only because shorter instructions reduce the number of reasoning loop iterations, which is what credits are billed against."), ("D", "The justification is flawed: credits are billed per user session regardless of instruction length, so no amount of trimming changes the bill.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Agent credit billing is tied to action executions (Apex, Flow, and similar calls), not to token or character counts. Token budget concerns instruction length for latency and LLM accuracy — a separate lever from cost.\n\nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: There is no per-character or per-token billing model for instruction text; credits are billed per action execution.  \n* C: Reasoning loop iterations are not themselves billed — only the action executions that occur within them are.  \n* D: Credits do scale with the number of action executions in a turn, so instruction length still indirectly matters for latency even though it does not change the credit bill."),
